@page "/docs/registry/getting-started"
<PageTitle>Registry: Getting started - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Getting started</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Registry</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">This guide takes a folder of components to a URL other projects can install from. It needs four commands and a static host to serve the result.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Lay out the source</h2>
        <p>A registry is an ordinary repository. The manifest, <code>registry.json</code>, sits at the root. Item files can live wherever you like, as long as the paths in the manifest are relative to the manifest itself. Only the build output gets hosted.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/Layout" Language="text" />
        <p>A single list of items is fine to start with. When it grows too long, split it with <code>include</code>, which folds a manifest per folder into the root one, as described on the <a href="docs/registry/registry-json">manifest reference</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Write the components</h2>
        <p>Declare your components under the <code>Blaizio.Ui</code> root namespace, the same convention the official registry uses. On install the CLI rewrites that prefix to the consumer's namespace (<code>MyApp.Components.Ui.&lt;YourRegistry&gt;</code>), so cross-references between your own components keep compiling wherever they land. <a href="docs/registry/author-a-component">Author a component</a> walks one component from the first line to the install, with the conventions that make it fit.</p>
        <p>Reference the headless layer (<code>Blaizio.Base</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons.Tabler</code>) as NuGet dependencies on the item. Never copy Base sources into a registry, because the consumer already has that package and a copied primitive stops receiving fixes.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Generate, validate, build</h2>
        <p>For a tree of components, <CmdChip Command="blaizio generate" /> writes <code>registry.json</code> for you, with one item per component folder. It infers dependencies between your own components from the <code>Bz*</code> types each file references, and names the official components you build on so you can add them as <code>@("@default/<item>")</code>. Writing the manifest by hand works too, and is easier for themes.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/BuildValidate" />
        <p><CmdChip Command="blaizio registry validate" /> is the check worth putting in CI. It confirms that the manifest parses, that names are unique, that theme and font items carry their payloads, and that every declared file exists on disk. <CmdChip Command="blaizio build" /> then inlines each file's contents into a per-item JSON and writes the <code>index.json</code> catalog beside them.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Host the output</h2>
        <p>Anything that serves files over HTTPS will do, because the CLI only ever issues GET requests for <code>&lt;base&gt;/index.json</code> and <code>&lt;base&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.json</code>. A GitHub Pages workflow that validates and then builds on every push is the whole pipeline:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/Workflow" Language="yaml" />
        <p>If you expect browser tooling to read the output, serve it with permissive CORS headers as well. A local folder path also works as a registry, which is the fastest way to test one before publishing it anywhere.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Try it as a consumer</h2>
        <p>To confirm everything works, point a scratch project at the built output, either the deployed URL or the local directory, and install an item. If the files land and the project builds, the registry is ready. What consumers do from here is covered on <a href="docs/registry/namespaces">Namespaces</a>.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/ConsumerAdd" />
    </section>
</div>
